Author: [LegitBaby](https://github.com/legitbaby)

Another challenge with some basic math!

Write a program that takes a single command line argument representing an integer between 1-100 inclusive and outputs "lucky" if the given number contains at least one digit that equals 7.

For example, the output should look like this:

```bash
$./solution 73
lucky
$./solution 12
not lucky
$./solution 7
lucky
```

Note that the digits don't have to appear consecutively. Also note that your solution needs to work correctly regardless of the position of the digits within the number. For instance, `73`, `37`, and `77` would all print "lucky".

### Solution:

```java
package pwnable;

import java.util.Arrays;

class Main {
	static boolean flag;

	private static void solve() throws Exception {
		if (args[0].equals("--help")) {
			throw new Exception("Usage:./Main [number]\n");
		}
		try {
			int num = Integer.parseInt(args[0]);
			boolean found = false;
			for (int i : Arrays.stream(num+"").chars().mapToObj(c -> c).toArray(Integer[]::new)){
				if (i==7){
					found = true;
					break;
				}
			}
			if (found){
				writer.print("lucky\n");
			}else{
				writer.print("not lucky\n");
			}
		}catch (Exception e){
			throw new Exception("Invalid Argument.\n");
		}
	}

	public static void main(final String[] args) throws Exception {
		scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
		writer = new PrintWriter(System.out);
		while (true) {
			try {
				solve();
			} catch (Exception e) {
				e.printStackTrace();
				writer.print(e.getMessage());
			} finally {
				writer.flush();
			}
		}
	}
}
```

