This piece of code is used to calculate the number of right triangles formed by two sides of an n-gon with integer side lengths from a given set of points. It takes as input an array `a` containing all possible pairs of integers `(x_i, y_i)` such that `|x_i|`, `|y_i|` are less than or equal to some constant `n`. The output will be the total number of right triangles formed by any pair of these points. In other words, it computes the sum over every pair of indices `i` and `j` where `i < j` of the area of the triangle defined by the three points `(0, 0)`, `(a[i].first, a[i].second)`, `(a[j].first, a[j].second)`. Note that since there could be duplicate elements in the input list, we must take care not to count each triangle twice when computing the answer. We can do so by keeping track of which triangles have already been counted via a hashmap mapping tuples of coordinates to booleans. For example, suppose our input was `[(-2, -1), (-2, 1), (3, 0)]`. Then after sorting the input list lexicographically, we would get the sorted list `[[(-2, -1), (-2, 1), (3, 0)]]`. Notice how the first point `-2,-1` appears before both `-2, 1` and `3, 0`. This means that the triplet formed by those three points defines one unique right triangle. Since we only want to count each triangle once, we keep track of whether we have seen each tuple of coordinates beforehand. If we see the same coordinate again, then we know that we have already counted its corresponding triangle previously, so we skip counting it again. After doing this, we compute the area of each triangle, add them up, and divide by 2 because we don’t need to consider the mirror image triangle. Finally, we modulo by `10^9+7` to avoid overflowing the value. Here is my implementation of the solution:

```java
package com.company;

import java.util.*;

class Main {
    private static Scanner sc;
    private static int N, K;
    private static Point[] pts;
    private static HashMap<Point, Boolean> map;
    private static HashSet<Integer> visited;
    private static int result;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        N = sc.nextInt();
        K = sc.nextInt();
        pts = new Point[N];
        map = new HashMap<>();
        visited = new HashSet<>();
        result = 0;

        // Read in points
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            pts[i] = new Point(sc.nextInt(), sc.nextInt());
        }

        Arrays.sort(pts);

        // Compute areas of all valid triangles
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < N; j++) {
                if (!visited.contains(i) &&!visited.contains(j)) {
                    visited.add(i);
                    visited.add(j);
                    if (!map.getOrDefault(pts[i], false) &&!map.getOrDefault(pts[j], false)) {
                        boolean flag = true;
                        for (int k = 0; k < N; k++) {
                            if (k!= i && k!= j) {
                                if (pts[i].distanceToSqr(pts[k]) > pts[j].distanceToSqr(pts[k])) {
                                    flag = false;
                                    break;
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        if (flag) {
                            result++;
                            map.put(pts[i], true);
                            map.put(pts[j], true);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // Divide by 2 to account for symmetry
        result >>= 1;

        // Modularize result
        result %= (int) Math.pow(10, 9) + 7;

        // Output
        System.out.print(result);
    }
}

